---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'Elation migration and daily billing tools · Kenechi Ucheagwu';
const description = 'An EHR migration to Elation, then claim checks and front-desk eligibility summaries built around Elation’s web app.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/projects/elation-billing-automation/">
  <header class="page-header">
    <div class="shell">
      <h1>Elation migration and daily billing tools</h1>
      <p class="subtitle project-status">in production</p>
      <p class="case-lede">I run IT and billing for a family-medicine practice in Jersey City. In January 2026 I led our move from Amazing Charts to Elation: data migration, vendor coordination and cutover. Once we were live, I built tools around Elation's web app that check claims before they're queued and make eligibility results readable at the front desk. These tools are part of the practice's daily work.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <article class="article shell">
    <section class="article-section" id="problem">
      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <p>I am the practice's only biller. Claims could go out with missing modifiers, unpriced charge lines or out-of-date coverage. Front-desk staff had no quick way to read copay, deductible and other-insurance details from an eligibility response.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section" id="checks">
      <h2>Claim checks</h2>
      <p>A claim goes through 14 checks before it's queued, for problems like blank charge lines and missing diagnosis links. A pricing check stops any charge line that has no price. After that, I do the final review and submit.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Built, used to review billed claims:</strong> a check for pairs of procedure codes that Medicare's coding rules say shouldn't be billed together (NCCI).</li>
        <li><strong>In use since October 2026:</strong> a read-only connection to Elation's API.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section" id="eligibility">
      <h2>A front-desk view of benefits</h2>
      <p>I turn the insurer's electronic eligibility response into a view of copay, coinsurance, deductible, out-of-pocket amounts and plan dates. The view uses the office-visit copay, so an emergency-room copay does not show up as the office copay.</p>
      <p>The dashboard sorts results into active coverage, no coverage, unclear and failed lookups. Staff can accept, edit or reject a result. The dashboard also flags other-coverage problems and differences between the chart and the insurer's response.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section" id="result">
      <h2>Result</h2>
      <p>The eligibility dashboard has run on the front-desk computer since August 17, 2026. The 14 claim checks and the pricing check are part of the daily billing run. The repository has more than 14,000 automated tests, counted on October 3, 2026.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section" id="next">
      <h2>What's next</h2>
      <p>Two more kinds of checks are built and tested but not yet part of the daily run: whether procedure (CPT) and diagnosis (ICD-10) codes were valid on the date of service, and whether the fee schedule has drifted. Adding them is the next step, so fewer claims come back.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section" id="stack">
      <h2>Stack</h2>
      <p>Python, Playwright, SQLite, pytest, a JavaScript Chrome extension and PowerShell.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
